WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.408
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.708 --> 00:00:08.944
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.244 --> 00:00:14.787
Unit seven: Fronting for Emphasis. Today you will move words to the front for effect.

4
00:00:15.087 --> 00:00:17.704
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:21.900
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:23.554
Fronting.

7
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.502
Fronting means moving a word to the start of a sentence.

8
00:00:28.117 --> 00:00:29.478
Fronting adds drama.

9
00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:32.178
Your turn. Say: Fronting.

10
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:35.791
Fronting.

11
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:37.679
Never have I.

12
00:00:38.683 --> 00:00:41.438
Never have I means a fronted negative with inversion.

13
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.073
Never have I felt so alive.

14
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:47.440
Your turn. Say: Never have I.

15
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.661
Never have I.

16
00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:53.580
Rarely does.

17
00:00:54.583 --> 00:00:57.314
Rarely does means a fronted negative with inversion.

18
00:00:57.917 --> 00:00:59.591
Rarely does it snow here.

19
00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:02.940
Your turn. Say: Rarely does.

20
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.242
Rarely does.

21
00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:09.054
Inversion.

22
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:13.164
Inversion means reversing the subject and the auxiliary.

23
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.993
Inversion follows a fronted negative.

24
00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:19.154
Your turn. Say: Inversion.

25
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:22.989
Inversion.

26
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.285
Emphatic word order.

27
00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:30.129
Emphatic word order means an unusual order that gives emphasis.

28
00:01:30.717 --> 00:01:32.194
Emphatic word order.

29
00:01:32.783 --> 00:01:35.888
Your turn. Say: Emphatic word order.

30
00:01:38.733 --> 00:01:40.466
Emphatic word order.

31
00:01:41.217 --> 00:01:42.276
Front position.

32
00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:45.724
Front position means the start of the sentence.

33
00:01:46.317 --> 00:01:48.293
The front position is powerful.

34
00:01:48.883 --> 00:01:51.582
Your turn. Say: Front position.

35
00:01:54.433 --> 00:01:55.562
Front position.

36
00:01:56.317 --> 00:01:57.654
Stylistic effect.

37
00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.112
Stylistic effect means an impression created by style.

38
00:02:02.717 --> 00:02:04.159
A stylistic effect.

39
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.855
Your turn. Say: Stylistic effect.

40
00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:12.142
Stylistic effect.

41
00:02:12.883 --> 00:02:14.302
Literary style.

42
00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:18.605
Literary style means the style of books and poems.

43
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:20.647
A literary style.

44
00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.169
Your turn. Say: Literary style.

45
00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:28.592
Literary style.

46
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.341
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:34.183 --> 00:02:37.959
Visal, why would a writer begin with Into the room walked the king?

48
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:48.729
This is fronting. The adverbial Into the room moves to the front for emphasis, and the subject comes after the verb. It creates drama and sets a scene.

49
00:02:49.817 --> 00:02:51.677
Is inversion always needed?

50
00:02:52.783 --> 00:03:07.571
After a fronted negative, yes: Not a word did he say. After a fronted adverbial of place, usually with be or motion verbs: Here comes the bus. With pronoun subjects, no inversion: Here he comes.

51
00:03:08.683 --> 00:03:10.079
What are the dangers?

52
00:03:11.183 --> 00:03:21.396
Overuse. One fronted structure can lift a paragraph, but ten will sound artificial. Choose the moment that deserves the extra emphasis.

53
00:03:22.533 --> 00:03:24.208
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:30.474
Grammar focus: Fronting: fronted element plus verb and auxiliary plus subject.

55
00:03:30.774 --> 00:03:31.996
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.683 --> 00:03:34.369
Into the room walked the king.

57
00:03:35.283 --> 00:03:36.865
Not a word did he say.

58
00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.239
Here comes the bus.

59
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:46.800
Front a word for emphasis. After a negative or place adverbial, the verb often comes before a noun subject.

60
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.562
Be careful! Don't say: Not a word he said.

61
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.859
Say: Not a word did he say.

62
00:03:55.159 --> 00:03:57.055
Invert after a fronted negative.

63
00:03:57.867 --> 00:04:00.519
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:01.333 --> 00:04:02.834
A Dramatic Entrance.

65
00:04:03.134 --> 00:04:05.226
Into the hall walked the new director.

66
00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:08.291
Not a single word did she say.

67
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.031
Rarely does a speech hold so much attention.

68
00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:15.277
On the table lay a mysterious letter.

69
00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:19.450
Fronting gives each sentence a dramatic, literary feeling.

70
00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:24.603
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:27.159
Not a word blank he say.

72
00:04:27.459 --> 00:04:30.207
A, did. B, does. C, do.

73
00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:35.778
The answer is A: did.

74
00:04:36.917 --> 00:04:38.266
Here blank the bus.

75
00:04:38.566 --> 00:04:42.196
A, the comes. B, comes. C, come it.

76
00:04:45.733 --> 00:04:47.570
The answer is B: comes.

77
00:04:48.717 --> 00:04:50.507
Which sentence uses fronting?

78
00:04:50.807 --> 00:04:55.506
A, She walked in.. B, In walked the king.. C, The king has walked..

79
00:04:59.067 --> 00:05:01.124
The answer is B: In walked the king..

80
00:05:02.283 --> 00:05:04.306
Fronting is common in blank writing.

81
00:05:04.606 --> 00:05:09.049
A, literary. B, text-message. C, grocery-list.

82
00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.774
The answer is A: literary.

83
00:05:15.917 --> 00:05:18.357
According to the reading, what lay on the table?

84
00:05:18.657 --> 00:05:21.939
A, a letter. B, a book. C, a key.

85
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.163
The answer is A: a letter.

86
00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:31.716
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:35.362
Write a sentence using fronting to add emphasis.

88
00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:45.569
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:45.869 --> 00:05:57.081
Into the silent classroom walked the director. Not a single student dared to speak. I used fronting to create a dramatic scene, and the inversion helps the reader feel the tension.

90
00:05:57.381 --> 00:05:59.043
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:06.689
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and fronting: fronted element plus verb and auxiliary plus subject.

92
00:06:06.989 --> 00:06:11.998
Today's value is appreciation of beauty. Learn to notice and value the beauty around you.

93
00:06:12.298 --> 00:06:14.695
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:06:14.995 --> 00:06:18.344
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

